# 103blackmagic_read_confirm_sdi.patch
#
# WHY THIS PATCH EXISTS
# ---------------------
# riscv_fault.md F5 / 12.6 / 12.8: on the SDI single-wire transport a read the debug module never
# performed is answered with the *previous* answer while `busy` and `cmderr` both stay clear, so
# no status bit a caller can poll reports it and BMP 102 - which re-issues what a path *reports* -
# can never fire on one. Measured on a CH32V003 over SDI: one whole 16-byte block per read, its
# data registers reading back 0x00000000, in ~2% of reads in a healthy session and a large
# fraction of them in a marginal one, silently. gdb sees it as compare-sections MIS-MATCHED on
# the first verify and matched on the second.
#
# WHAT IT DOES
# ------------
# * riscv_dmi_s gains `read_confirm`, set by the SDI DMI only (swindle/src/template/sdi_template.h
#   sets it in the DMI factory). A DMI that can report per access (JTAG, RVSWD, ADI) is untouched,
#   so a healthy target's traffic is byte-identical - the confirmed read is selected by the
#   transport, not imposed on every part the way BMP 98 proposed. Patch 98's two-pass scheme is
#   otherwise reused verbatim (window 128 B, element 4 B, three attempts).
# * riscv32_mem_read() reads a window twice on such a transport and only accepts a window the two
#   passes agree on. A window they disagree about is settled element by element (4 B) - T11's
#   measured confirmation granularity - and BMP 102's re-issue of a *reported* failure still wraps
#   the whole call.
# * The end-of-transfer check minichlink ends its bulk reads with (DefaultWaitForDoneOp(dev,0)
#   after DefaultReadBinaryBlob): one read of abstractioncs, and a latched command error makes the
#   transfer a reported failure instead of silently handing back whatever DATA0 held. The
#   streaming reader (BMP 86) never waits for the command it starts, so this is the only place
#   that can see an autoexec'd access fault.
# * The command error is cleared *with a read-back*, bounded: a clear that did not land leaves the
#   module ignoring every command after it, which is how one dropped frame becomes a whole window
#   of faults (BMP 102's "one 13 KB window in three faults, the same window again is clean").
# * abstractauto is written through one place that confirms the write on an untrusted transport
#   (read it back, write it again, bounded): a lost *drop* leaves the stream armed, and the a0/a1
#   restores that follow re-run a stale command.
# * `mon riscv_stream on|off` selects the streaming sub-path at runtime and `mon riscv_confirm`
#   prints the counters the confirmed read keeps: windows, disagreements, elements, faults,
#   latched command errors, abstractauto retries, and the last disagreement with both answers.
#   This is the A/B and the instrumentation riscv_fault.md 12.9 is measured with.
# * The diagnostics that have to be readable on the probe go to Logger() behind the existing
#   `mon memlog on` switch (bmp_mem_log_enabled): ENABLE_DEBUG is 0 on the embedded builds, so
#   every DEBUG_WARN() on these paths (BMP 100's GPR restore, BMP 102's re-issue) compiles to
#   *nothing* on the probe.
#
